  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "We call a permutation†^{\\dag}† PPP of length NNN as KKK-sortable if it is possible to **sort** the permutation (in non-decreasing order) using the following operation **at most** once :\n\n\nChoose a subset of indices from [1,N][1, N][1,N];\nAdd KKK to the elements present at all those indices in PPP.\nGiven NNN and a permutation PPP of length NNN, find the **sum** of all KKK where:\n\n\n1≤K≤N1\\le K\\le N1≤K≤N;\nPPP is KKK-sortable.\n†^{\\dag}† A permutation of length NNN is an array of NNN distinct elements which are all in the range [1,N][1, N][1,N]. For example, [2,3,1,4][2, 3, 1, 4][2,3,1,4] is a permutation of length 444 whereas [2,4,3][2, 4, 3][2,4,3] and [1,1,2][1, 1, 2][1,1,2] are not\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains one integer, NNN - the length of the permutation.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers, P1,P2,…,PNP_1, P_2, \\ldots, P_NP1​,P2​,…,PN​ - the permutation PPP.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, sum of all KKK such that PPP is KKK-sortable and 1≤K≤N1 \\le K \\le N1≤K≤N.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤5⋅1041 \\le T \\le 5 \\cdot 10^41≤T≤5⋅104",
      "1≤N≤5⋅1051 \\le N \\le 5 \\cdot 10^51≤N≤5⋅105",
      "1≤Pi≤N1 \\le P_i \\le N1≤Pi​≤N",
      "Pi≠PjP_i \\ne P_jPi​=Pj​ for all 1≤i&lt;j≤N1 \\le i \\lt j \\le N1≤i&lt;j≤N.",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 5⋅1055 \\cdot 10^55⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
